Aether reputation enforcement for OPNsense / FreeBSD, on pf
===========================================================

Port of net/aether-sensord from the optim-wrt feed. The nftables mechanism
becomes pf; the enforcement *proof* is preserved and strengthened.

WHAT THIS PACKAGE CURRENTLY SHIPS
---------------------------------
aether-sensord-pfcanary -- the enforcement canary. It answers one question
without reading any configuration: is this firewall actually blocking traffic
from the reputation table, or does the table merely exist?

Exit status is 0 only for `enforced`. Every other verdict, including
`inconclusive`, exits non-zero, so a monitor cannot be fooled by "could not
check".

    192.0.2.199  <-- the canary address (TEST-NET-1, cannot carry real traffic)

Verdicts, and what each means:

    enforced          the MAIN ruleset references the table; a rule is in the
                      datapath
    set_missing       the table does not exist -- the declaration never landed
    add_rejected      pfctl refused the element
    not_held          pfctl accepted it and the table does not list it
    not_enforced      THE ONE THIS EXISTS TO CATCH: the table exists and holds
                      the entry, and no rule refers to it, so nothing is ever
                      dropped
    cleanup_failed    enforcement works but the canary was left behind
    inconclusive      the check could not be run

`not_enforced` is the failure this whole mechanism exists for. Nothing errors.
The table lists its contents. Every other signal agrees that enforcement is
live. It is what an anchor-based OPNsense plugin produces by construction,
because OPNsense's generated ruleset contains zero anchor lines.

THE ENFORCEMENT SEAM
--------------------
Do NOT load reputation rules into a pf anchor. OPNsense regenerates its ruleset
from config.xml on every filter reload, so an anchor's rules exist, print
correctly under `pfctl -a <name> -s rules`, and are never evaluated.

Register the table as an OPNsense static alias of type `external` instead:

    /usr/local/opnsense/mvc/app/models/OPNsense/Firewall/static_aliases/core.json

    {
      "aisense_rep4": {
        "enabled": "1",
        "name": "aisense_rep4",
        "type": "external",
        "description": "AIsense reputation feed (aether-sensord)",
        "expire": "3600",
        "content": ""
      }
    }

OPNsense then generates the rule referencing it. Precedent: `sshlockout` is
referenced 4 times in the generated ruleset and `virusprot` 3 times. `expire`
gives kernel-side decay, so a feed that stops refreshing stops blocking instead
of leaving a stale table enforcing forever.

Populate it out of band:

    pfctl -t aisense_rep4 -T add 203.0.113.7

Precedent to copy: /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/syslog/lockout_handler, which
populates `sshlockout`.

TWO MEASURED TRAPS
------------------
Both were hit building this port. Each gives a confidently wrong answer, and
they pull in opposite directions.

1. pf does not report a drop to the sender. On Linux netfilter a blocked output
   packet returns EPERM to the socket, and the original daemon used that as
   proof. pf discards silently -- measured with the canary held in an enforcing
   table: sends=10 refused=0. So a sendto()-based probe reports NOT_ENFORCED
   against a perfectly defending firewall, and an operator who learns to ignore
   that verdict has lost the only signal that matters. The verdict here comes
   from pf's own state, never from the send result.

2. The per-table `Rules:` count in `pfctl -vvsT` is NOT enforcement evidence. It
   counts rules mentioning the table anywhere, including inside an anchor the
   main ruleset never calls. Measured state where it lies:

       References:  [ Anchors: 1   Rules: 1  ]              <- looks enforced
       pfctl -s rules | grep -c '<aisense_rep4>'  ->  0      <- nothing live

   A canary trusting that count reports ENFORCED on a firewall that blocks
   nothing -- fabricated protection, the worst possible error. This port checks
   the MAIN ruleset for a `<table>` reference instead.

   The deliberate cost: a hand-written config that enforces from a *called*
   anchor reads as NOT_ENFORCED. That trade is correct -- a missed enforcement
   prompts a look, an invented one does not -- and it does not affect the
   static-alias design above, whose rule is generated into the main ruleset.

Also note: `pfctl -F all` does NOT flush anchors. Between test cases, run
`pfctl -a <name> -F rules` for each entry in `pfctl -s Anchors`, or a stale
anchor keeps supplying reference counts that make the next case look enforced.

WHAT PORTS FROM THE OPENWRT PACKAGE
-----------------------------------
    ports unchanged (pure C):     nft rendering logic -> src/pf.c
                                 canary logic       -> src/canary_pf.c
                                 policy/sigdb/match + appdb data
    ports, different mechanism:  nftables sets  -> pf tables + static aliases
                                 NFLOG          -> pflog
                                 UCI /etc/config -> OPNsense model + config.xml
                                 procd init     -> rc.d

    does NOT port:
      kmod-aether-af   a Linux kernel module hashing TLS SNI / HTTP host for
                       in-kernel name matching. FreeBSD has no equivalent hook,
                       so the name-hash half of enforcement has no datapath
                       here. Address and tuple enforcement via pf is unaffected.
      libndpi          linked on OpenWrt for protocol identification. Its value
                       is classification, not enforcement. Left out of the first
                       cut so the package carries no LGPL dependency it does not
                       yet use.

BUILDING
--------
    ./build-pkg.sh /path/to/aether-sensord-checkout [outdir]
    pkg add <outdir>/aether-sensord-pf-0.1.0.pkg

No ports tree required. The tests run first and a failing test stops the build.

VERIFYING AN INSTALL
--------------------
    aether-sensord-pfcanary aisense_rep4          # exit 0 only when enforcing
    aether-sensord-pfcanary aisense_rep4 --spool /var/spool/aether

`--spool` writes the verdict as NDJSON where the uplink will find it, written to
a .partial and renamed so a consumer never reads half a record. It is emitted on
every run, not only when the verdict changes: the controller treats an absent
verdict as an alarm, so the steady stream of passes is the heartbeat that makes
silence meaningful.
